WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.980
Parameterization prevents bound values
from altering SQL syntax,

2
00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.174
but a secure data operation requires
additional decisions.

3
00:00:07.254 --> 00:00:10.979
Runtime validation establishes whether a
value has an acceptable type,

4
00:00:11.059 --> 00:00:13.440
range, length, and meaning for the
operation.

5
00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.038
Authorization establishes whether the
authenticated subject may perform that action

6
00:00:18.118 --> 00:00:19.217
on the targeted data.

7
00:00:19.297 --> 00:00:23.388
Business rules establish whether the
change is permitted in the current state.

8
00:00:23.468 --> 00:00:27.559
A parameterized query can be perfectly
resistant to injection and still expose

9
00:00:27.639 --> 00:00:30.692
another tenant's records or apply an
unauthorized update.

10
00:00:30.772 --> 00:00:32.542
Carry trusted tenant, ownership,

11
00:00:32.622 --> 00:00:36.652
or membership scope into the query rather
than accepting it from the request as

12
00:00:36.732 --> 00:00:37.342
authority.

13
00:00:37.422 --> 00:00:41.819
Validate search limits and date ranges so
callers cannot request unreasonable work.

14
00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:44.341
Check workflow and entitlement before
writes.

15
00:00:44.421 --> 00:00:47.474
These controls complement binding rather
than replace it.

16
00:00:47.554 --> 00:00:50.729
A validated email is still unsafe when
concatenated into SQL,

17
00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:54.594
and a safely bound document ID still
requires an authorization decision.

18
00:00:54.674 --> 00:00:57.605
Naming each control makes tests and review
more precise.

19
00:00:57.685 --> 00:01:01.837
Database least privilege limits
consequences when application code or another

20
00:01:01.917 --> 00:01:02.710
control fails.

21
00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:06.454
Use an application account with only the
tables, schemas, operations,

22
00:01:06.534 --> 00:01:08.610
and procedures that the workload needs.

23
00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:11.621
A reporting service may need read access
without writes;

24
00:01:11.701 --> 00:01:16.036
a narrowly scoped worker may need one
procedure rather than broad ownership rights.

25
00:01:16.116 --> 00:01:19.657
Avoid administrator credentials for
routine application traffic,

26
00:01:19.737 --> 00:01:23.217
and separate workloads when their
privilege requirements differ.

27
00:01:23.297 --> 00:01:25.984
Least privilege cannot remove an injection
defect,

28
00:01:26.064 --> 00:01:29.361
but it can reduce the data and operations
reachable through one.

29
00:01:29.441 --> 00:01:33.470
Handle database errors as an operational
and information-exposure concern.

30
00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:37.458
Return stable client messages that do not
reveal unnecessary schema names,

31
00:01:37.538 --> 00:01:39.370
query text, or connection details,

32
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.541
while preserving useful diagnostics in
protected server logs with secrets and

33
00:01:43.621 --> 00:01:45.025
sensitive values removed.

34
00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:49.318
Hiding errors does not prevent injection
and must not replace parameterization.

35
00:01:49.398 --> 00:01:53.183
Together, binding, validation,
authorization, business enforcement,

36
00:01:53.263 --> 00:01:57.415
narrow database privileges, and
disciplined error handling create layers with

37
00:01:57.495 --> 00:01:58.533
distinct purposes.

38
00:01:58.613 --> 00:02:01.849
Their separation allows each layer to be
implemented, tested,

39
00:02:01.929 --> 00:02:06.020
and monitored without pretending that one
control solves every database risk.
